entry.summary:
An uptime check answers one question: did the server respond? Visitors ask a different one: can I use this page? After a few years of looking after client sites, the gap between those two answers was the thing I hit most often. A CSS deploy left a blank white screen. A WordPress plugin update broke checkout. An SSL certificate expired while the uptime dashboard still showed green.
I am Rai Ansar, a developer and DevOps engineer in Islamabad. I have worked on websites and servers since May 2019, and I built Visual Sentinel to close that gap. Here is what the visual part taught me.
The core of a visual monitor fits in a few lines. This is a minimal sketch with Playwright, not our production code:
import { chromium } from 'playwright';

const browser = await chromium.launch();
const page = aw…

When you build a checkout form, you need card numbers to test it. The usual shortcut is to type 4111 1111 1111 1111 or make up some digits. That works until it doesn't, so here is what actually matters.
1. Luhn check. Almost every card number passes the Luhn checksum. Your front-end validation (and the payment processor) rejects numbers that don't. If you type random digits, about 9 out of 10 fail before your real code even runs, and you end up debugging the wrong thing.
Here is the check in a few lines:
function isLuhnValid(input) {
  const value = input.replace(/\D/g, '') // strips spaces and dashes
  if (!value) return false

  let sum = 0
  let double = false
  for (let i = value.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    let digit = Number(value[i])
